## Before class

Today is a working session, and it only works if you arrive with a draft. Two things are due **before class starts**:

1. **Upload your proposal draft** to <course-link type="assignment" id="cp3-draft">the draft turn-in</course-link>. The deadline is set to the start of the class period. It's **completion-checked, not scored** — it exists so that your revision has something to be measured against.
2. **Bring two printed copies of that draft.** Print them yourself. Your reviewers mark them up on paper, in pen, and you take both copies home.

**Minimum draft:** problem & users, solution, non-goals, technical approach, and risks. A rough *full* draft earns you a much better review than four polished sections.

**Re-skim (5 minutes):** the convergent-thinking principles in the [CPS handbook](https://brdo.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/cps_handbook.pdf), **pp. 6–10**. Reviewing someone else's scope is convergent thinking applied to a problem that isn't yours — affirmative judgment, deliberate criteria, and no "yes, but" reflexes.

**Also bring:** a pen. A **review protocol sheet** — with the <course-link type="page" id="pg-rubrics">CP3 rubric</course-link> on it — is handed out in class, and is posted here: <file path="../../resources/Review-Protocol-Sheet.pdf" canvas_folder="Course Handouts" />

> **This one is graded downstream.** <course-link type="assignment" id="cp3">CP3</course-link>'s *Argument & revision* criterion measures your visible response to feedback, so **no draft on file caps that criterion at Developing** — there's nothing to show improvement against. And a classmate who reviews an empty desk loses their round too.

