## Purpose

Work that only its author has read is a guess.

Every engineering organization you'll ever join has some version of today built into it: a design review, a code review, an RFC comment period, a pull request that needs an approval before it merges. The reason isn't bureaucracy — it's that the author is the one person who *cannot* see the gap, because they've been filling it in from memory for weeks. Someone who owes you nothing and knows nothing has to read it cold.

Today your proposal gets two of those readers, and you become one for two other people. Both jobs are graded skills, and the second one transfers further than students expect: giving precise, useful, kind feedback is most of what senior engineers do all day. *(In this course you'll do it again on design documents in mid-October, on code and pull requests the week after, and on a classmate's whole repository in November.)*

---

## Learning objectives

By the end of this session, you'll be able to:

1. **Review a peer's proposal against explicit criteria** — scope realism, risk honesty, evidence quality — rather than against your taste.
2. **Give feedback specific enough to act on** — the difference between "this seems like a lot" and "scope realism — I don't believe the calendar sync fits five weeks of real building."
3. **Receive feedback professionally** — capture it, probe it, and decide *later* what to act on.
4. **Narrow an overscoped proposal** into a defensible one-semester vertical slice.
5. **Turn a pile of review notes into a ranked edit list** you can actually execute this weekend.

---

## What we'll do together

We start with the protocol, because unstructured review produces compliments. Three moves: **read silently** with the rubric in front of you and a pen on the author's page, starting with scope realism and risks — the two criteria where first drafts fail; **mark three things in writing** — the strongest section and why, your sharpest concern named in the rubric's language, and one concrete suggestion the author could act on this week; then **discuss**, with the author asking and capturing rather than explaining. Initial your comments: the author submits these pages, and the grader should be able to see whose feedback got acted on.

Then two rounds with different partners. Both of your readers come in cold — that's how the pairings are built — so the second round isn't a harsher audience, it's an **independent** one, and that changes what you can conclude. A concern *both* readers raise is about the proposal; fix it first. A concern only one raises is ambiguous — worth checking with the other reader rather than discarding. (It's the same inference you'll make when two reviewers leave the same comment on a pull request.) Round two adds one more question, the one that actually decides next Wednesday: **"Would this pitch recruit you?"**

We close with a **scope-narrowing clinic**, because the most common finding will be *too big*. Four moves — cut the platform and keep the slice, demote features to non-goals, shrink the user, defer the hard integration — applied live to a volunteered proposal or two, then checked against the arithmetic out loud. Narrowing is not retreating: a proposal that names a smaller thing and proves it is stronger than one that promises a platform and ships a login page.

---

## What you'll leave with

- **Two marked-up copies of your draft**, in two different hands, with three written notes on each and an answer to whether your idea would recruit its reader.
- **A ranked list of three concrete edits**, starting with whatever both readers flagged — plus the one narrowing move your proposal most needs.
- **An honest read on your scope.** Say the arithmetic out loud: the **~10 hours a week is the total course load** — class, reading, discovery, writing, and reviews all come out of it — so subtract before you multiply, and multiply by **roughly five weeks of real building**, not fourteen. Most overscoped proposals die on that line. Better here, on paper, than in Week 12.
- **Practice at the reviewer's register**, which is the part that shows up in your <course-link type="assignment" id="cp9">Technical Communication Portfolio</course-link> later this term and in every code review you'll ever leave.

### Tonight

**Capture both annotated copies while you still know which is which.** Scanning gives the cleanest result — a scanner, or a scanning app on your phone — but a plain photo is perfectly acceptable as long as it's **legible**: flat page, good light, your reviewer's handwriting readable. Both images are part of Monday's submission.

Then revise. Start at the top of your edit list, not with the easiest fix.

